\title{The Rapidity Geometry of Confidence Intervals for Correlations,\\
and the Resolution Floor of a Fading Dial}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-09-01}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A rank-correlation measurement is reported at the largest setting of a monotone
experimental ladder: three seeds return Spearman correlations $0.562$, $0.551$, $0.582$,
pooling to $0.565$ with a reported $95\%$ interval $[0.542,0.587]$, against a
pre-registered validation floor of $0.55$. All readings clear the floor; one clears it by
$+0.001$; every interval reaches below the floor at its lower end.

We develop the exact geometry of the \emph{interval}, rather than the point estimate, and
use it to score the record. Writing $\zet(r)=\artanh r$ for the rapidity of a correlation
and $d(x,y)=(x-y)/(1-xy)$ for the relativistic difference, we prove:
(i) $\zet(x)-\zet(y)=\zet(d(x,y))$, so rapidity gaps of correlations are themselves
correlations; (ii) a rapidity-symmetric interval of half-width parameter $\tau$ about a
reading $r$ is exactly $[d(r,\tau),d(r,-\tau)]$, with width law
$2\tau(1-r^2)/(1-r^2\tau^2)$ and asymmetry law --- the lower arm exceeds the upper by
exactly $2r\tau^2(1-r^2)/(1-r^2\tau^2)$, strictly positive at any positive reading;
(iii) a certification criterion in closed form, $\tau\le d(r,f)$; and (iv) the resolution
law: with Fisher half-width $z/\sqrt{n-3}$, a floor $f$ is certified if and only if
$n\ge 3+\big(z/(\zet(r)-\zet(f))\big)^2$. We further prove that rapidity is canonical: it
is the unique coordinate, up to affine change, in which the interval half-width does not
depend on the reading.

Applying the machinery: the reported interval is reproduced to better than $6\times10^{-4}$
by a single rapidity half-width parameter $\tau=0.033$ and by no correlation-symmetric
interval; the experiment therefore carries between $3400$ and $3650$ effective paired
draws; certifying the pooled reading over the floor requires at least $7900$ draws;
the $+0.001$ clearance requires at least $1.8\times10^{6}$; and no individual seed
certifies the floor. A rapidity-linear extrapolation places the crossing strictly between
settings $82$ and $83$ and the next reading in $(0.543,0.545)$, and certifying that drop
would cost at least $74{,}000$ draws --- more than twenty times the present budget.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

Consider an experiment parameterised by a discrete knob $b$ (here: the bit-length of
uniformly drawn integers). At each setting one measures the Spearman rank correlation
$\rho_T(b)$ between a statistic $T$ of the drawn integer --- the number of trailing zeros
in its binary expansion --- and a downstream \emph{rate}. A validation band $[0.55,0.85]$
was pre-registered: the dial is said to \emph{hold} at setting $b$ when $\rho_T(b)$ lies in
the band.

At the highest uniform setting, $b=80$, three independent seeds return

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
seed & $\rho_T$\\
\midrule
A & $0.562$\\
B & $0.551$\\
C & $0.582$\\
pooled & $0.565$, CI $[0.542,0.587]$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Three further facts are recorded: all readings lie inside the band; seed B clears the floor
$0.55$ by only $+0.001$; and every reported interval reaches below $0.55$ at its lower end.
A secondary comparison --- the advantage of $T$ over a plain population-count baseline ---
persists, with pooled advantage $+0.053$ (interval $[0.030,0.083]$), placing the baseline
at $0.512$. The next setting, $b=84$, is announced as a \emph{crossing test}.

\subsection{The gap this paper fills}

Every previous analysis of this ladder has treated the reading as a \emph{point}. But the
record's three most consequential statements are all statements about \emph{intervals}: a
$+0.001$ margin, a lower end below the floor, and a band decision. No point-estimate
analysis can adjudicate any of them. The missing layer is the exact shape of a
rapidity-symmetric interval once mapped back to correlation coordinates, together with the
sample-size cost of resolving a stated margin. This paper builds that layer from first
principles, proves it canonical, and applies it.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, correlations live in the open interval $(-1,1)$.

\begin{definition}[Rapidity]
For $x\in(-1,1)$, the \emph{rapidity} of $x$ is
\[
\zet(x)=\artanh x=\tfrac12\log\frac{1+x}{1-x},
\]
with inverse $x=\tanh\zet$. Equivalently $2\zet(x)=\log\frac{1+x}{1-x}$; this doubled form
turns a rapidity into the logarithm of a rational number whenever $x$ is rational.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Relativistic difference]
For $x,y\in(-1,1)$, $\displaystyle d(x,y)=\frac{x-y}{1-xy}$. This is the velocity
subtraction law of special relativity with $c=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rapidity-symmetric interval]
Let $r\in(-1,1)$ be a reading and $h>0$ a rapidity half-width; put $\tau=\tanh h\in(0,1)$.
The associated interval is $I(r,\tau)=[L(r,\tau),U(r,\tau)]$ with
\[
L(r,\tau)=\frac{r-\tau}{1-r\tau},\qquad U(r,\tau)=\frac{r+\tau}{1+r\tau}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fisher half-width and required samples]
At confidence multiplier $z>0$ and effective sample size $n>3$,
$h(z,n)=z/\sqrt{n-3}$, and for a margin $M>0$, $N(z,M)=3+(z/M)^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Certification]
A measurement with reading $r$ and half-width parameter $\tau$ \emph{certifies} the floor
$f$ when $f\le L(r,\tau)$; it \emph{certifies a drop below} the ceiling $f$ when
$U(r,\tau)\le f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rapidity-linear model]
Given rungs $(b_1,r_1)$, $(b_2,r_2)$, the model reading at $b$ is
\[
\widehat r(b)=\tanh\!\Big(\zet(r_1)+\tfrac{b-b_1}{b_2-b_1}\big(\zet(r_2)-\zet(r_1)\big)\Big),
\]
and the crossing setting for a floor $f$ is
$b^{*}=b_1+\dfrac{(\zet(r_1)-\zet(f))(b_2-b_1)}{\zet(r_1)-\zet(r_2)}$.
\end{definition}

\section{Rapidity algebra}

\begin{lemma}[Logarithmic form]\label{lem:log}
For $-1<x<1$, $\zet(x)=\tfrac12(\log(1+x)-\log(1-x))$ and $2\zet(x)=\log\frac{1+x}{1-x}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from $\artanh x=\log\sqrt{(1+x)/(1-x)}$ and the logarithm laws, both denominators
being positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Range of the relativistic difference]\label{lem:range}
If $x,y\in(-1,1)$ then $1-xy>0$ and $d(x,y)\in(-1,1)$; moreover $d(x,y)>0$ iff $y<x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$|xy|<1$ gives $1-xy>0$. Then $d(x,y)<1$ is equivalent to $(1+y)(1-x)>0$ and $d(x,y)>-1$ to
$(1-y)(1+x)>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rapidity differences are correlations]\label{thm:sub}
For all $x,y\in(-1,1)$, $\ \zet(x)-\zet(y)=\zet\big(d(x,y)\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compute $1+d(x,y)=\frac{(1+x)(1-y)}{1-xy}$ and $1-d(x,y)=\frac{(1-x)(1+y)}{1-xy}$.
Substituting into Lemma~\ref{lem:log} and splitting the logarithms of products and
quotients (all factors positive) yields the identity after cancellation of $\log(1-xy)$.
\end{proof}

The rapidity coordinate therefore converts relativistic subtraction of correlations into
ordinary subtraction. Every statement about differences of rapidities is equivalently a
statement about a single correlation, which is what makes closed-form criteria possible.

\begin{lemma}[Elementary two-sided bounds]\label{lem:bounds}
For $0\le x<1$,
\[
\frac{x(2+x)}{2(1+x)}\ \le\ \zet(x)\ \le\ \frac{x(2-x)}{2(1-x)}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both follow from $\log t\le t-1$ alone. Upper: apply at $t=1+x$ to get $\log(1+x)\le x$, and
at $t=1/(1-x)$ to get $-\log(1-x)\le x/(1-x)$; halve the sum. Lower: apply at $t=1/(1+x)$
and at $t=1-x$ and halve the sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Rapidity dominates correlation]\label{prop:dom}
For $0\le x<1$, $\ x\le\zet(x)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The crude bounds of Lemma~\ref{lem:bounds} are provably too weak: the gap
$\zet(x)-x=x^3/3+O(x^5)$ is cubic, while $\log t\le t-1$ is only first-order accurate.
Instead let $g(t)=\log(1+t)-\log(1-t)-2t$ on $(-1,1)$. Then
$g'(t)=\frac{1}{1+t}+\frac{1}{1-t}-2=\frac{2t^2}{1-t^2}\ge0$, so $g$ is monotone on $[0,x]$;
with $g(0)=0$ this gives $2\zet(x)\ge 2x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is structural rather than technical: variance stabilisation is a genuinely analytic,
not algebraic, phenomenon. No first-order comparison of logarithms can deliver the sharp
inequality.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Rapidity never deflates an advantage]\label{cor:adv}
For $0\le b\le a<1$, $\ a-b\le\zet(a)-\zet(b)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sub} the right side is $\zet(d(a,b))\ge d(a,b)$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:dom}, and $d(a,b)=(a-b)/(1-ab)\ge a-b$ since $0<1-ab\le1$.
\end{proof}

\section{The geometry of a rapidity-symmetric interval}

\begin{proposition}[Endpoints are back-transforms]\label{prop:endpoints}
For $r,\tau\in(-1,1)$,
$L(r,\tau)=\tanh(\zet(r)-\zet(\tau))$ and $U(r,\tau)=\tanh(\zet(r)+\zet(\tau))$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$L(r,\tau)=d(r,\tau)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sub} and $\tanh\circ\zet=\mathrm{id}$. For
$U$, note $U(r,\tau)=d(r,-\tau)$ and $\zet(-\tau)=-\zet(\tau)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Width law]\label{thm:width}
For $r,\tau\in(-1,1)$,
\[
U(r,\tau)-L(r,\tau)=\frac{2\tau(1-r^2)}{1-r^2\tau^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put both endpoints over the positive common denominator $(1+r\tau)(1-r\tau)=1-r^2\tau^2$;
the numerator simplifies to $2\tau(1-r^2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Asymmetry law]\label{thm:asym}
For $r,\tau\in(-1,1)$,
\[
\big(r-L(r,\tau)\big)-\big(U(r,\tau)-r\big)=\frac{2r\tau^2(1-r^2)}{1-r^2\tau^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Same common denominator; the numerator collects to $2r\tau^2(1-r^2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A positive reading always dips further down than up]\label{cor:dip}
If $0<r<1$ and $0<\tau<1$ then $U(r,\tau)-r<r-L(r,\tau)$.
\end{corollary}

The record's observation that ``every interval dips below the floor at its lower end'' is
therefore forced geometry: given a positive reading and the standard construction, the
lower arm is always the longer one. The observation contains no information about the dial
beyond what the point estimate already carries.

\begin{theorem}[Certification criterion]\label{thm:cert}
Let $r,\tau\in(-1,1)$ and $f\in[0,1)$. Then $f\le L(r,\tau)\iff\tau\le d(r,f)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both $1-r\tau$ and $1-rf$ are positive (Lemma~\ref{lem:range}), so both inequalities may be
cleared of denominators; each reduces to the same polynomial inequality
$\tau(1-rf)\le(r-f)(1-r\tau)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mirror criterion for a ceiling]\label{thm:ceil}
Let $r\in(-1,1)$, $0\le\tau<1$, $f\in(-1,1)$. Then $U(r,\tau)\le f\iff\tau\le d(f,r)$.
\end{theorem}

Certifying a drop below a level therefore costs precisely the same rapidity margin as
certifying a clearance above it.

\section{The resolution law}

\begin{lemma}[Half-width versus sample size]\label{lem:hw}
For $z>0$, $n>3$, $M>0$: $\ h(z,n)\le M\iff N(z,M)\le n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$z/\sqrt{n-3}\le M\iff z/M\le\sqrt{n-3}\iff (z/M)^2\le n-3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Resolution law]\label{thm:res}
Let $z>0$, $n>3$, $0\le f<r<1$, and $\tau=\tanh h(z,n)$. Then
\[
f\le L(r,\tau)\iff n\ \ge\ 3+\left(\frac{z}{\zet(r)-\zet(f)}\right)^{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} the left side is $\tau\le d(r,f)$; since $\tanh$ and $\zet$ are
inverse strictly increasing bijections, this is $h(z,n)\le\zet(d(r,f))=\zet(r)-\zet(f)$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:sub}. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:hw}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Quadratic cost law]\label{cor:quad}
For $M\neq0$ and any $k$: $N(z,M/k)-3=k^{2}(N(z,M)-3)$; and $N(z,\cdot)$ is strictly
decreasing on $(0,\infty)$.
\end{corollary}

The resolution law is a dictionary: on the left an experimental verdict, on the right a
single number --- the rapidity margin, squared and inverted.

\section{Canonicity: rapidity is the only stabilising coordinate}

A sample correlation from $n$ paired draws has asymptotic variance $(1-\rho^2)^2/n$. Under
a smooth reparameterisation $g$ the delta method gives $g'(\rho)^2(1-\rho^2)^2/n$;
demanding that this be free of $\rho$ --- which is precisely the condition making an
interval half-width reading-independent --- is the differential equation
\[
g'(x)(1-x^2)=c,\qquad x\in(-1,1).
\]

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:deriv}
For $x\in(-1,1)$, $\zet'(x)=\dfrac{1}{1-x^2}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Canonicity of rapidity]\label{thm:canon}
Suppose $g$ is differentiable on $(-1,1)$ with $g'(x)=c/(1-x^2)$ there. Then
$g(x)=c\,\zet(x)+g(0)$ for all $x\in(-1,1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $h(t)=g(t)-c\,\zet(t)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:deriv}, $h'\equiv0$ on the convex open set
$(-1,1)$, so $h$ is constant; evaluate at $0$ where $\zet(0)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Two-way form]
$g$ satisfies $g'(x)=c/(1-x^2)$ on $(-1,1)$ if and only if $g(x)=c\,\zet(x)+b$ there for
some constant $b$.
\end{corollary}

Rapidity is thus not one convenient chart among many: it is the unique chart, up to affine
change, in which the width of an interval is not a function of the thing being measured.

\section{Scoring the record}

Set $z=1.96$, $f=0.55$, $r_{\text{pool}}=0.565$, $r_A=0.562$, $r_B=0.551$, $r_C=0.582$.

\begin{proposition}[Band membership; pooling consistency]
All three seed readings and the pooled reading lie strictly inside $[0.55,0.85]$, and the
pooled reading equals the arithmetic mean of the three seeds to within $10^{-4}$ (indeed
exactly).
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The reported interval is rapidity-symmetric]\label{thm:u80sym}
With $\tau=0.033$,
\[
|L(0.565,0.033)-0.542|<6\times10^{-4},\qquad |U(0.565,0.033)-0.587|<6\times10^{-4}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$L=(0.565-0.033)/(1-0.565\cdot0.033)=0.542107\ldots$ and
$U=(0.565+0.033)/(1+0.565\cdot0.033)=0.587054\ldots$, exact rational evaluations.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No correlation-symmetric interval matches]
$U(0.565,0.033)-0.565<0.565-L(0.565,0.033)$, and $L(0.565,0.033)<0.55$. The arm gap is
$\approx 8.4\times10^{-4}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Effective sample size]\label{thm:eff}
The effective number of paired draws carried by the reported interval,
$N(1.96,\zet(0.033))$, satisfies $3400\le n_{\text{eff}}\le3650$ (true value $\approx3528$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:bounds} at $x=0.033$ and transfer the resulting bounds through the
monotonicity of $N(z,\cdot)$.
\end{proof}

No external record of the sample size is needed: the interval itself reveals it.

\begin{theorem}[The pooled reading is undersampled]\label{thm:under}
Certifying $f=0.55$ from $r=0.565$ requires
$n\ge N(1.96,\zet(0.565)-\zet(0.55))\ge7900$; hence $2n_{\text{eff}}<n_{\text{required}}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The margin equals $\zet(d)$ with $d=0.015/(1-0.31075)=0.021756\ldots$; the upper bound of
Lemma~\ref{lem:bounds} gives $N\ge 3+(1.96/0.022)^2>7900$ (exact value $\approx8112$).
Combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:eff}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The $+0.001$ clearance is statistically empty]\label{thm:empty}
Certifying $f=0.55$ from $r_B=0.551$ requires $n\ge1.8\times10^{6}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$d(0.551,0.55)=0.001/(1-0.30305)=0.0014349\ldots$, so
$N\ge3+(1.96/0.001435)^2>1.8\times10^{6}$ (exact value $\approx1.866\times10^6$).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No seed certifies the floor]\label{thm:noseed}
With each seed carrying at most $n_{\text{eff}}/3\le1217$ draws,
$n_{\text{eff}}/3<N(1.96,\zet(r_s)-\zet(0.55))$ for $s\in\{A,B,C\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The three required sizes admit rigorous lower bounds $12{,}500$, $1.8\times10^{6}$ and
$1650$ (exact values $\approx12{,}735$, $1.866\times10^{6}$, $1735$), each exceeding
$1217$.
\end{proof}

The best seed, $0.582$, fails by a factor of about $1.4$; the others by factors of $10$
and $1500$. The claim ``the dial holds'' is a claim about a point estimate; its substantive
version is that the certification set of the measurement contains the floor, and at this
budget it does not.

\section{Count parity in the natural coordinate}

\begin{theorem}[Rapidity inflates the advantage]\label{thm:parity}
$\zet(0.565)-\zet(0.512)\ge0.0745$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sub} the left side is $\zet(d)$ with
$d=0.053/(1-0.28928)=0.074574\ldots$, and $\zet(d)\ge d$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:dom}.
Exact value $0.07471$.
\end{proof}

The raw advantage $+0.053$ becomes at least $+0.0745$ in the canonical coordinate: an
inflation of about $40\%$.

\begin{theorem}[But the advantage still fades]\label{thm:fade}
At the much lower setting $b=44$, the corresponding readings were $0.78$ against a $0.71$
baseline, and
\[
1.8\big(\zet(0.565)-\zet(0.512)\big)<\zet(0.78)-\zet(0.71).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper-bound the left factor by $\zet(d(0.565,0.512))\le0.0776$ and lower-bound the right by
$\zet(d(0.78,0.71))\ge0.1462$, both via Lemma~\ref{lem:bounds}; then
$1.8\times0.0776=0.1397<0.1462$. Exact values $0.0747$ and $0.1582$.
\end{proof}

The fade is therefore genuine and not an artefact of a compressive scale.

\section{The crossing prediction, and what the crossing test costs}

The two most recent rungs are $(72,0.605)$ and $(80,0.565)$.

\begin{lemma}[Rational reduction]\label{lem:rat}
Using $2\zet(x)=\log\frac{1+x}{1-x}$:
\[
2(\zet(0.605)-\zet(0.565))=\log\tfrac{27927}{24727},\quad
2(\zet(0.565)-\zet(0.55))=\log\tfrac{939}{899},\quad
2(\zet(0.605)-\zet(0.55))=\log\tfrac{2889}{2449}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\frac{1+0.605}{1-0.605}=\frac{321}{79}$, $\frac{1+0.565}{1-0.565}=\frac{313}{87}$,
$\frac{1+0.55}{1-0.55}=\frac{31}{9}$; take quotients.
\end{proof}

Because $\log$ is strictly increasing, any integer-coefficient linear inequality between
these rapidity gaps is exactly an inequality between products of rational powers.

\begin{theorem}[The floor is crossed between settings 82 and 83]\label{thm:cross}
$82<b^{*}<83$, where
$b^{*}=72+\dfrac{8(\zet(0.605)-\zet(0.55))}{\zet(0.605)-\zet(0.565)}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Clearing the positive denominator, the two bounds are
$10(\zet(0.605)-\zet(0.565))<8(\zet(0.605)-\zet(0.55))<11(\zet(0.605)-\zet(0.565))$,
which by Lemma~\ref{lem:rat} are exactly
\[
\left(\tfrac{27927}{24727}\right)^{5}<\left(\tfrac{2889}{2449}\right)^{4}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\left(\tfrac{2889}{2449}\right)^{8}<\left(\tfrac{27927}{24727}\right)^{11},
\]
both decidable in exact rational arithmetic. Numerically $b^{*}\approx82.86$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The predicted reading at setting 84]\label{thm:84}
$0.543<\widehat r(84)<0.545$; in particular $\widehat r(84)<0.55$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\widehat r(84)=\tanh(\zet(0.605)+\tfrac{12}{8}(\zet(0.565)-\zet(0.605)))$. Since $\tanh$ is
strictly increasing, compare rapidity arguments; doubling and using
$\frac{1+0.543}{1-0.543}=\frac{1543}{457}$, $\frac{1+0.545}{1-0.545}=\frac{309}{91}$, the
comparisons become
\[
\left(\tfrac{1543}{457}\right)^{2}\tfrac{321}{79}<\left(\tfrac{313}{87}\right)^{3}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\left(\tfrac{313}{87}\right)^{3}<\left(\tfrac{309}{91}\right)^{2}\tfrac{321}{79}.
\]
Numerically $\widehat r(84)\approx0.54393$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete crossing, no continuity assumed]\label{thm:disc}
Let $(w_k)$ satisfy $w_{k+1}\le w_k-\delta$ for a fixed $\delta>0$. Then
$w_k\le w_0-k\delta$, and $w_k\le L$ whenever $k\ge(w_0-L)/\delta$. With
$w_0=\zet(0.565)$ and $\delta=\zet(0.605)-\zet(0.565)$ one gets $w_1<\zet(0.55)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Linear decay is induction on $k$. The application needs
$2\zet(0.565)-\zet(0.605)<\zet(0.55)$, which after doubling and exponentiating is exactly
$\left(\tfrac{313}{87}\right)^{2}<\tfrac{31}{9}\cdot\tfrac{321}{79}$.
\end{proof}

One further $8$-bit rung at the observed fade rate therefore already puts the dial below
the floor, with no continuity assumption in the setting.

\begin{theorem}[Pricing the crossing test]\label{thm:price}
Certifying that the reading has dropped below $0.55$ requires $n\ge38{,}000$ at the
optimistic end $0.543$ and $n\ge74{,}000$ at the conservative end $0.545$; consequently
$20\,n_{\text{eff}}<n_{\text{required}}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:ceil} the cost is $N(1.96,\zet(0.55)-\zet(\widehat r))$; bound the
rapidity margins above using Lemma~\ref{lem:bounds} applied to $d(0.55,\widehat r)$ and use
monotonicity of $N$. Exact values $\approx38{,}600$ and $\approx75{,}300$. Combine with
$n_{\text{eff}}\le3650$.
\end{proof}

Run at the present budget, the announced crossing test cannot be decisive whatever it
returns.

\section{The identifiability barrier}

Suppose a dial fades approximately linearly in rapidity,
$\zet(\rho(b))\approx\zet_0-\lambda(b-b_0)$ with $\lambda>0$. Then relative to a fixed floor
$f$ the margin $M(b)=\zet(\rho(b))-\zet(f)$ shrinks linearly in $b$, while by
Corollary~\ref{cor:quad} the cost of resolving it grows as $M(b)^{-2}$. Setting the cost
equal to a budget $N$,
\[
M\ge\frac{z}{\sqrt{N-3}}\iff b\le b_{\max}(N):=b_0+\frac{1}{\lambda}
\Big(\zet_0-\zet(f)-\frac{z}{\sqrt{N-3}}\Big).
\]

Hence there is a \emph{last decidable rung}: for each budget $N$ a largest setting at which
any classification of the dial relative to the floor is possible; beyond it, an experiment
of size $N$ returns readings whose certification sets simply do not contain the floor,
regardless of which side of it the truth lies on. Concretely, on this ladder the pooled
margin fell from $\approx0.0616$ in rapidity at setting $72$ to $\approx0.0208$ at setting
$80$, a factor of $3$, which by the quadratic cost law multiplied the certification cost by
$9$.

\section{Algorithms}

All three primitives run in $O(1)$ arithmetic operations per call.

\paragraph{Algorithm A --- Interval reconstruction.}
Input: reported endpoints $(\ell,u)$. Set $\zet_\ell=\artanh\ell$, $\zet_u=\artanh u$,
$r=\tanh\frac{\zet_\ell+\zet_u}{2}$, $\tau=\tanh\frac{\zet_u-\zet_\ell}{2}$,
$n=3+(2z/(\zet_u-\zet_\ell))^2$. Correctness is
Proposition~\ref{prop:endpoints}: the rapidity midpoint and half-gap invert the
construction exactly.

\paragraph{Algorithm B --- Certification test.}
Input: $r$, $\tau$, floor $f$. Compute $g=d(r,f)$; certified iff $\tau\le g$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}); the shortfall factor is $(\artanh\tau/\artanh g)^2$, the multiple
by which $n$ must grow (Corollary~\ref{cor:quad}).

\paragraph{Algorithm C --- Ladder extrapolation and crossing.}
Input: two rungs, a floor, a target setting; output $\widehat r(b)$ and $b^{*}$. For
rational readings all comparisons reduce via Lemma~\ref{lem:rat} to comparisons of products
of rational powers with integer exponents, settled exactly.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\paragraph{Reporting practice.} Any report of a correlation with an asymmetric interval
implicitly declares a rapidity half-width; Algorithm A extracts it and with it the
effective sample size. Interval asymmetry should never be reported as a finding: by
Corollary~\ref{cor:dip} it is forced.

\paragraph{Pre-registration design.} The resolution law converts the smallest
scientifically interesting reading into a required $n$. Designs that cannot meet
$3+(z/(\zet(r)-\zet(f)))^2$ should not be run against that floor.

\paragraph{Meta-analysis.} Because rapidity gaps are themselves correlations
(Theorem~\ref{thm:sub}), differences between studies can be reported on the same scale as
the studies themselves, and Corollary~\ref{cor:adv} guarantees such reporting never
understates an effect relative to raw differences.

\paragraph{A limitation.} The analysis takes the reported interval and multiplier at face
value and treats $n_{\text{eff}}$ as the quantity implied by them; genuine effective sample
sizes for rank correlations under ties differ from the nominal count, and the constant in
the asymptotic variance for Spearman's coefficient differs from the Pearson case by a
factor depending on the underlying copula. These refinements change the constants but not
the structure: cost remains the inverse square of a rapidity margin.

\section{Future directions}

Two conjectures organise the next steps.

\begin{conjecture}[The identifiability barrier is quadratic in the fade]
As a monotone dial approaches a pre-registered floor, its rapidity margin shrinks linearly
in the setting while the sample size needed to resolve it grows like the inverse square of
that margin. There is therefore a last decidable rung $b_{\max}(N)$ beyond which no
experiment of size $N$ can classify the dial relative to the floor, and $b_{\max}$ grows
only like $\log N$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Rapidity-metric geodesy of the ladder]
Once rapidity is known to be the canonical coordinate, the ladder becomes a curve in a
one-dimensional hyperbolic metric space, and ``the dial fades linearly'' is the statement
that the ladder is a geodesic parametrised by arclength. Deviations from linearity are then
curvature, a coordinate-free quantity, computable from the recorded ladder and comparable
against the seed-spread noise.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

A correlation is a velocity; rapidity is its proper coordinate, and provably the only one.
In that coordinate a confidence interval is a symmetric segment, and mapping it back to
correlation space produces two exact laws --- a width law and an asymmetry law --- that
between them account for the qualitative features of the record without reference to the
experiment at all. Certification of a floor is then a single inequality between a
half-width and a relativistic gap, and its sample-size form is the resolution law.

Applied to the measurement in question, the machinery returns an unambiguous verdict. The
experiment carries at most about $3650$ effective draws. It needs at least $7900$ to
certify its pooled reading over the floor, at least $12{,}500$ for its strongest seed at
the seed-level budget, and at least $1.8$ million for the seed whose $+0.001$ clearance was
recorded as a success. Not one of the three seeds certifies the floor. The extrapolation
puts the crossing between settings $82$ and $83$ and the next reading in $(0.543,0.545)$;
certifying that drop would cost more than twenty times the present budget.

The dial did not hold at setting $80$, and it did not fail. At this budget, it was not
measured.

